PART 2 — GOOGLE CORE QUESTION
❓ “Why does modifying a list inside a function affect the caller?”

Let’s walk through it step by step.
==============================
Example
def add_item(lst):
    lst.append(100)

my_list = [1, 2, 3]
add_item(my_list)
print(my_list)
=============================
Output
[1, 2, 3, 100]

🧠 What actually happens (THIS IS WHAT GOOGLE WANTS)
my_list is a reference to a list object
When passed to the function, the reference is copied
Both caller and function point to the same object
append() modifies the object in place


👉 No new list is created.
⭐ Google-level explanation
“Lists are mutable, and Python passes object references to functions. When we modify a list inside a function, we’re modifying the same underlying object that the caller references, so the change is visible outside.”



👉  Modification vs reassignment — Google loves this distinction.

def update_list(lst):
    lst = [10, 20]

my_list = [1, 2, 3]
update_list(my_list)
print(my_list)


Ans [1,2,3]

Why?
lst = [10, 20] creates a new list
The local reference is reassigned
The original list is untouched


👉  PART 4 — DEFAULT ARGUMENT TRAP
def add_item(item, lst=[]):
    lst.append(item)
    return lst

print(add_item(1))
print(add_item(2))
print(add_item(3))

[1]
[1, 2]
[1, 2, 3]

🧠 Why this happens
Default arguments are evaluated once
lst is shared across function calls
It persists between invocations

✅ Google-approved fix
def add_item(item, lst=None):
    if lst is None:
        lst = []
    lst.append(item)
    return lst

⭐ Interview explanation
“Mutable default arguments are evaluated once at function definition time, which can cause shared state across calls. I avoid this by using None and initializing inside the function.”

