✅ Q1. Why is this dangerous?
Code
def foo(x, cache={}):
    cache[x] = True
Your answer
because cache is one list object but gain we add as dictionary
You sensed shared object, which is good 👍
But let’s sharpen it.
❌ What’s actually wrong (clear explanation)
cache is a dictionary, not a list
Default arguments are created once, not per call
This dictionary is shared across all function calls
🧠 What really happens
foo(1)  # cache = {1: True}
foo(2)  # cache = {1: True, 2: True}
foo(3)  # cache = {1: True, 2: True, 3: True}
Even if you didn’t want caching — it happens silently.
🔴 Why Google considers this dangerous
Hidden shared state
Unexpected behavior
Hard-to-debug production bugs
Thread-safety issues
✅ Google-ready answer
“This is dangerous because the default dictionary is shared across function calls. Since default arguments are evaluated once, the cache persists between calls, creating hidden state and unexpected side effects.”
✅ Correct fix (Google-approved)
def foo(x, cache=None):
    if cache is None:
        cache = {}
    cache[x] = True

