✅ Q2. Difference between lst.copy() and lst[:]
You said:
not sure
That’s very normal. Let’s lock it in clearly.
🔹 Key truth (important!)
👉 For lists, lst.copy() and lst[:] are equivalent
Both create a shallow copy.
Example
lst = [1, 2, 3]

a = lst.copy()
b = lst[:]
a and b are new lists
But inner objects are shared
Proof
lst = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]
a = lst.copy()

a[0].append(99)
print(lst)
Output
[[1, 2, 99], [3, 4]]
Why?
Outer list copied
Inner lists shared
✅ Google-ready answer
“Both lst.copy() and slicing create a shallow copy of the list. The outer container is copied, but nested mutable objects are still shared.”

Both copies reflect the change because they point to the same inner lists.
Difference in practice
.copy() is more readable and explicit.
[:] is more flexible (works on strings, tuples, etc.).
Performance is nearly identical for lists.
